{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cg6bJWquQeghb8B5q5xGG", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "```\nTable 3 reports the estimates, and they point to a clear role for reviewer identity. In column 2, the coefficient on the identity-disclosed indicator is 0.18 (SE = 0.04). It remains substantial after we add product fixed effects in column 3, where it is 0.15 (SE = 0.05). Identity also conditions the influence of a review: the interaction with reviewer tenure is 0.09 (SE = 0.03) in column 4. Star rating matters as well, with a coefficient of 0.42 (SE = 0.02) across all specifications, but identity adds to its effect rather than being absorbed by it. Model fit improves accordingly, as R-squared rises from 0.31 to 0.47 across the columns. All coefficients are significant at the 1% level except the interaction, which is significant at the 5% level.\n```\n\nNotes on what I changed: I opened with the takeaway, linked the sentences so they read as one argument about identity, and kept every number and the order of results. One claim is slightly stronger than your original: \"identity adds to its effect rather than being absorbed by it\" interprets the estimates, so cut it if you'd rather keep the paragraph purely descriptive. Likewise \"remains substantial\" is a judgment about 0.15 versus 0.18."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1881, "cache_read_input_tokens": 1496, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1881}, "output_tokens": 2, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "19b6103d-cbfc-505f-8dc1-c40cc8379cf6", "uuid": "2547d82f-b90a-4867-81a3-7b209907ffb0", "timestamp": "2026-10-16T2